# df — real-world examples

## Free space, human readable

```sh
df -h
```

## Show filesystem type too

```sh
df -Th
```

## Homelab: just the media server pool

```sh
df -h /srv/plex /home
```

## Homelab: check inode exhaustion (disk "full" but space free)

```sh
df -i
```

## Exclude pseudo-filesystems (tmpfs noise)

```sh
df -h -x tmpfs -x devtmpfs
```

## Count files per directory (find inode hogs)

```sh
echo "Detailed inode usage for: $(pwd)"
for d in $(find -maxdepth 1 -type d | cut -d/ -f2 | grep -xv . | sort); do
  c=$(find "$d" | wc -l)
  printf "%s\t\t- %s\n" "$c" "$d"
done
printf "Total: \t\t%s\n" "$(find "$(pwd)" | wc -l)"

# Quicker one-liners
find . -printf "%h\n" | cut -d/ -f-2 | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn
find / -xdev -printf '%h\n' | sort | uniq -c | sort -k 1 -n
```

| Flag | What it does                     |
| ---- | -------------------------------- |
| -h   | human readable sizes             |
| -T   | show filesystem type             |
| -i   | inode usage instead of blocks    |
| -x   | exclude a filesystem type        |
